    "lawCode": "Amd §50-i, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "Court of Claims Act",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3325\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the court of claims act and the general municipal law,\nin relation to extending the time to file a notice of claim for\ncertain persons who sustained injury as a result of the World Trade\nCenter rescue, recovery or cleanup operations\n\n PURPOSE :\nTo extend the time for filing a notice of claim for injuries suffered\nas a result of participation in the world Trade Center rescue,\nrecovery, or clean-up operations.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection one is the short title, \"Jimmy Nolan's Law\".\n\nSection two, Section 10 of the court of claims act is amended by\nadding a new subdivision 10.\n\nSection three, Subdivision 1 of section 50-e of the general municipal\nlaw is amended by adding a new paragraph (c).\n\nSection four is an immediate effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nWe all know that thousands of men and women lost their lives on\nSeptember 11, 2001 at the World Trade Center tragedy. Thousands of\nindividuals who worked at the site and at site-related areas for\nperiods of time that ran up to two and one-half years, were told and\nbelieved that they were working in a safe environment. However, today,\nafter more than five years, we are seeing that more and more\nindividuals are experiencing health problems that are far above the\nstatistical norm, leading any reasonable person to conclude that these\nillnesses are being caused by their 911-related rescue, recovery and\nclean-up efforts.  However, most of the people who are sick and are\nstill getting sick are beyond the current statutory ninety day limit\nfor filing a notice of claim. These individuals must not be denied\ntheir right to pursue just compensation simply because he or she was\ngiven incorrect information, had no immediate symptoms or was unaware\nof the filing time limits.  The 911 tragedy created this special group\nof 'second wave 911 victims'; this is a special case which must be\naddressed in a special way. The proposed bill would assure that all\nsecond wave 911 victims' involved with the rescue, recovery and\nclean-up would be treated with the dignity and equity that they\ndeserve. The proposed bill protects and preserves the rights of those\n911 workers who are sick as well as those that are continuing to get\nsick.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008: S.3177-A Referred to Judiciary\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nUnknown.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
